1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,492
A pipeline can finish successfully and still produce the wrong numbers.

2
00:00:05,492 --> 00:00:12,775
In Missing Revenue Lab, the source reports one hundred thousand euros, but the warehouse reports

3
00:00:12,775 --> 00:00:14,232
ninety five thousand.

4
00:00:14,232 --> 00:00:21,516
This fictional incident gives you the code, evidence, and tests to investigate the missing five

5
00:00:21,516 --> 00:00:22,487
thousand euros.

6
00:00:23,587 --> 00:00:29,682
Clone the repository and run python dash m lab serve dash dash open.

7
00:00:29,682 --> 00:00:34,839
The dashboard runs on your own computer with Python and SQLite.

8
00:00:34,839 --> 00:00:39,059
No Docker, cloud account, or API key is required.

9
00:00:39,059 --> 00:00:46,560
Read the source contract, inspect the evidence, and edit the indicated Python file in your editor.

10
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:51,905
The first delivery advances the checkpoint to ten o'clock.

11
00:00:51,905 --> 00:00:58,035
A different order arrives in the second delivery with that same source timestamp.

12
00:00:58,035 --> 00:01:05,110
The loader selects only timestamps strictly greater than the checkpoint, so it silently excludes the

13
00:01:05,110 --> 00:01:06,053
new order.

14
00:01:06,053 --> 00:01:11,712
Source update time does not tell you which identities have already arrived.

15
00:01:12,812 --> 00:01:18,400
Changing greater than to greater than or equal is not enough.

16
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:23,989
It can replay orders, and it still misses arbitrarily late records.

17
00:01:23,989 --> 00:01:29,578
Our source delivers complete extracts, so the reference processes every record.

18
00:01:29,578 --> 00:01:32,626
Order identity is a primary key.

19
00:01:32,626 --> 00:01:38,214
An upsert replaces an existing order only with a newer version.

20
00:01:38,214 --> 00:01:41,263
Duplicate deliveries no longer create revenue.

21
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:49,949
The reference now reports one hundred thousand euros, and all eight acceptance checks pass.

22
00:01:49,949 --> 00:01:56,994
The checks also exercise duplicate rows, repeated deliveries, corrected amounts, stale versions, and

23
00:01:56,994 --> 00:01:58,619
older new identities.

24
00:01:58,619 --> 00:02:02,954
Use the reference view to inspect the result.

25
00:02:02,954 --> 00:02:06,748
It does not replace your learner code.

26
00:02:06,748 --> 00:02:09,999
Your own repair must pass independently.

27
00:02:11,099 --> 00:02:14,701
The second incident is a reporting grain problem.

28
00:02:14,701 --> 00:02:22,356
One hundred and fifty euros of orders minus twenty euros of refunds should produce one hundred and

29
00:02:22,356 --> 00:02:23,257
thirty euros.

30
00:02:23,257 --> 00:02:29,111
But joining two items and two refunds creates four rows for one order.

31
00:02:29,111 --> 00:02:35,415
The query repeats the order amount and reports four hundred and ten euros overall.

32
00:02:36,515 --> 00:02:43,135
Aggregate refunds to one row per order before joining them to the orders table.

33
00:02:43,135 --> 00:02:47,391
Then sum each order amount minus its refund total.

34
00:02:47,391 --> 00:02:51,174
The item table is unnecessary for this metric.

35
00:02:51,174 --> 00:02:54,956
Do not use sum distinct on monetary values.

36
00:02:54,956 --> 00:03:00,158
Two legitimate orders or refunds can have exactly the same amount.

37
00:03:00,158 --> 00:03:02,522
All eight checks now pass.

38
00:03:03,622 --> 00:03:09,718
The third incident uses a fixed business offset of UTC plus two hours.

39
00:03:09,718 --> 00:03:16,753
An order at ten thirty PM UTC on June fourteenth belongs to June fifteenth locally.

40
00:03:16,753 --> 00:03:23,318
Another at ten fifteen PM UTC on June fifteenth belongs to the next day.

41
00:03:23,318 --> 00:03:29,884
Slicing the timestamp's date reports two hundred and fifty euros instead of three hundred.

42
00:03:30,984 --> 00:03:39,583
Parse a timezone aware datetime, convert it to the business offset, and compare its local date.

43
00:03:39,583 --> 00:03:43,882
Reject missing or invalid timezones instead of guessing.

44
00:03:43,882 --> 00:03:49,256
All eleven checks pass, including midnight boundaries and negative offsets.

45
00:03:49,256 --> 00:03:53,018
This exercise deliberately uses a fixed offset.

46
00:03:53,018 --> 00:04:00,004
Real regional calendars with daylight saving require named timezones and a timezone database.

47
00:04:01,104 --> 00:04:03,774
Now try the incidents yourself.

48
00:04:03,774 --> 00:04:10,180
Write down your hypothesis, repair the learner files, and rerun the checks.

49
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:14,984
Export your investigation notes and explain the remaining limitations.

50
00:04:14,984 --> 00:04:20,322
Missing Revenue Lab is published by YECA2K with Codex assistance.

51
00:04:20,322 --> 00:04:27,796
The data is synthetic, the challenges are free, and the reference solutions are included.
